# Line-ending policy.
#
# The launchers under diag/ and backends/corex/diag/ are submitted to a Linux cluster and
# run by bash. A CRLF there fails in the least diagnosable way this project has hit: the job
# runs for about two seconds, reports COMPLETED, and leaves a 0-byte log, because the first
# token of every line carries a trailing carriage return. It reads like a scheduler fault or
# a wedged card, and it costs a job to discover.
#
# Editing on Windows reintroduces CRLF unless git is told not to, so the rule is enforced at
# checkout time rather than left to the editor.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Python source is read by the interpreter, which tolerates CRLF, but keeping one convention
# avoids a spurious whole-file diff whenever a file crosses platforms.
*.py text eol=lf
*.cu text eol=lf
*.cpp text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
